"vocabulary": "crossref"}]}], "member": "1643", "event": {"name": "Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers)", "start": {"date-parts": [[2025, 7]]}, "location": "Vienna, Austria", "end": {"date-parts": [[2025, 7]]}}, "container-title": ["Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers)"], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2025, 8, 4]], "date-time": "2025-08-04T09:58:21Z", "timestamp": 1754301501000}, "score": 68.89037, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.1336"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2025]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.acl-long.1336", "published": {"date-parts": [[2025]]}}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 30]], "date-time": "2026-07-30T16:14:47Z", "timestamp": 1785428087144, "version": "3.56.0"}, "reference-count": 29, "publisher": "Optica Publishing Group", "issue": "17", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 3]], "date-time": "2026-06-03T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1780444800000}, "content-version": "vor", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1364/OA_License_v2#VOR"}, {"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 3]], "date-time": "2026-06-03T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1780444800000}, "content-version": "tdm", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://opg.optica.org/policies/opg-tdm-policy.json"}], "funder": [{"DOI": "10.13039/501100001809", "name": "National Natural Science Foundation of China", "doi-asserted-by": "crossref", "award": ["62075149"], "award-info": [{"award-number": ["62075149"]}], "id": [{"id": "10.13039/501100001809", "id-type": "DOI", "asserted-by": "crossref"}, {"id": "https://ror.org/01h0zpd94", "id-type": "ROR", "asserted-by": "publisher"}]}, {"name": "Qing Lan Project of Higher Learning Institutions in Jiangsu Province"}], "content-domain": {"domain": ["opg.optica.org"], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "short-container-title": ["Appl. Opt."], "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 10]]}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Multi-spot techniques play a key role in advancing parallel laser processing and high-speed imaging applications. However, the energy uniformity of multi-spot patterns is highly susceptible to non-uniform incident intensity, optical-system imperfections, and external disturbances. To address this critical challenge, we propose a feedback-control strategy aided by real-time detection, where the weighting coefficient of each focal spot is iteratively updated to obtain the desired multi-spot distribution. Experimental results demonstrate that the uniformity of a 2\u00d74 multi-spot array agrees well with the target distribution, with the intensity deviation of each individual spot from the mean intensity constrained within \u00b12%. Benefiting from the independent tunability of each focal spot\u2019s weighting coefficient, the proposed method further enables programmable intensity allocation among focal spots. Comparative laser ablation experiments are performed on a 100\u00a0nm thick chromium film utilizing a motorized 3D translation stage. Without modulation, the weakest focal spot only attains an ablation of 45\u00a0nm, which is insufficient to fully remove the chromium film. After feedback optimization, the processing depths at all spots were approximately 102\u00a0nm with greatly improved morphology consistency. Therefore, this method provides an effective approach to enhance the energy uniformity of multi-focus systems and generate user-defined intensity distributions, showing high potential for parallel laser processing and related optical applications.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1364/ao.589356", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 5, 18]], "date-time": "2026-05-18T13:00:07Z", "timestamp": 1779109207000}, "page": "5767", "update-policy": "https://doi.org/10.1364/crossmark_policy", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Precise multi-focu